[15.40995, "o", "   241\tIn this section the ‘sort’ commands are omitted for brevity.  Sorting\r\n   242\tthe files before joining is still required.\r\n   243\t\r\n   244\t   ‘join’’s default behavior is to print only lines common to both input\r\n   245\tfiles.  Use ‘-a’ and ‘-v’ to print unpairable lines from one or both\r\n   246\tfiles.\r\n   247\t\r\n"]
[15.410062, "o", "   248\tAll examples below use the following two (pre-sorted) input files:\r\n   249\t\r\n   250\t     $ cat file1                          $ cat file2\r\n   251\t     a 1                                  a A\r\n   252\t     b 2                                  c C\r\n   253\t\r\n   254\tCommand                              Outcome\r\n   255\t                                     \r\n   256\t--------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n   257\t     $ join file1 file2              common lines (_intersection_)\r\n   258\t     a 1 A                           \r\n"]
[15.410156, "o", "   259\t     $ join -a 1 file1 file2         common lines _and_ unpaired lines\r\n   260\t     a 1 A                           from the first file\r\n   261\t     b 2                             \r\n   262\t     $ join -a 2 file1 file2         common lines _and_ unpaired lines\r\n   263\t     a 1 A                           from the second file\r\n   264\t     c C                             \r\n   265\t     $ join -a 1 -a 2 file1 file2    all lines (paired and unpaired)\r\n   266\t     a 1 A                           from both files (_union_).\r\n"]
[15.410193, "o", "   267\t     b 2                             see note below regarding ‘-o\r\n   268\t     c C                             auto’.\r\n   269\t                                     \r\n   270\t     $ join -v 1 file1 file2         unpaired lines from the first file\r\n   271\t     b 2                             (_difference_)\r\n   272\t                                     \r\n   273\t     $ join -v 2 file1 file2         unpaired lines from the second\r\n"]
[15.410203, "o", "   274\t     c C                             file (_difference_)\r\n"]
[15.410937, "o", "   275\t                                     \r\n   276\t     $ join -v 1 -v 2 file1 file2    unpaired lines from both files,\r\n   277\t     b 2                             omitting common lines (_symmetric\r\n   278\t     c C                             difference_).\r\n   279\t                                     \r\n   280\t\r\n   281\tThe ‘-o auto -e X’ options are useful when dealing with unpaired lines.\r\n   282\tThe following example prints all lines (common and unpaired) from both\r\n   283\tfiles.  Without ‘-o auto’ it is not easy to discern which fields\r\n   284\toriginate from which file:\r\n   285\t\r\n   286\t     $ join -a 1 -a 2 file1 file2\r\n   287\t     a 1 A\r\n   288\t     b 2\r\n   289\t     c C\r\n   290\t\r\n   291\t     $ join -o auto -e X -a 1 -a 2 file1 file2\r\n   292\t     a 1 A\r\n   293\t     b 2 X\r\n   294\t     c X C\r\n   295\t\r\n   296\t   If the input has no unpairable lines, a GNU extension is available;\r\n   297\tthe sort order can be any order that considers two fields to be equal if\r\n   298\tand only if the sort comparison "]
[15.410999, "o", "described above considers them to be\r\n   299\tequal.  For example:\r\n   300\t\r\n   301\t     $ cat file1\r\n   302\t     a a1\r\n   303\t     c c1\r\n   304\t     b b1\r\n   305\t\r\n   306\t     $ cat file2\r\n   307\t     a a2\r\n   308\t     c c2\r\n   309\t     b b2\r\n   310\t\r\n   311\t     $ join file1 file2\r\n   312\t     a a1 a2\r\n   313\t     c c1 c2\r\n   314\t     b b1 b2\r\n   315\t\r\n   316\t8.3.5 Header lines\r\n   317\t------------------\r\n   318\t\r\n   319\tThe ‘--header’ option can be used when the files to join have a header\r\n   320\tline which is not sorted:\r\n   321\t\r\n   322\t     $ cat file1\r\n   323\t     Name     Age\r\n   324\t     Alice    25\r\n   325\t     Charlie  34\r\n   326\t\r\n   327\t     $ cat file2\r\n   328\t     Name   Country\r\n   329\t     Alice  France\r\n   330\t     Bob    Spain\r\n   331\t\r\n   332\t     $ join --header -o auto -e NA -a1 -a2 file1 file2\r\n   333\t     Name     Age   Country\r\n   334\t     Alice    25    France\r\n   335\t     Bob      NA    Spain\r\n   336\t     Charlie  34    NA\r\n   337\t\r\n   338\t   To sort a file with a header line, u"]
[15.411011, "o", "se GNU ‘sed -u’.  The following\r\n   339\texample sort the files but keeps the first line of each file in place:\r\n   340\t\r\n   341\t     $ ( sed -u 1q ; sort -k2b,2 ) < file1 > file1.sorted\r\n   342\t     $ ( sed -u 1q ; sort -k2b,2 ) < file2 > file2.sorted\r\n   343\t     $ join --header -o auto -e NA -a1 -a2 file1.sorted file2.sorted > file3\r\n   344\t\r\n   345\t8.3.6 Union, Intersection and Difference of files\r\n   346\t-------------------------------------------------\r\n   347\t\r\n   348\tCombine ‘sort’, ‘uniq’ and ‘join’ to perform the equivalent of set\r\n   349\toperations on files:\r\n   350\t\r\n   351\tCommand                              outcome\r\n   352\t--------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n   353\t‘sort -u file1 file2’                Union of unsorted files\r\n   354\t                                     \r\n   355\t‘sort file1 file2 | uniq -d’         Intersection of unsorted files\r\n   356\t                                     \r\n   357\t‘sort file1 file1 file2 | uniq -u"]
[15.411016, "o", "’   Difference of unsorted files\r\n   358\t                                     \r\n   359\t‘sort file1 file2 | uniq -u’         Symmetric Difference of unsorted\r\n   360\t                                     files\r\n   361\t                                     \r\n   362\t‘join -t '' -a1 -a2 file1 file2’     Union of sorted files\r\n   363\t                                     \r\n   364\t‘join -t '' file1 file2’             Intersection of sorted files\r\n   365\t                                     \r\n   366\t‘join -t '' -v2 file1 file2’         Difference of sorted files\r\n   367\t                                     \r\n   368\t‘join -t '' -v1 -v2 file1 file2’     Symmetric Difference of sorted\r\n   369\t                                     files\r\n   370\t                                     \r\n   371\t\r\n   372\t   All examples above operate on entire lines and not on specific\r\n   373\tfields: ‘sort’ without ‘-k’ and ‘join -t ''’ both consider entire lines\r\n   374\tas the key.\r\n   375\t\r\n   376\t2 Common opt"]
[15.411021, "o", "ions\r\n   377\t****************\r\n   378\t\r\n   379\tCertain options are available in all of these programs.  Rather than\r\n   380\twriting identical descriptions for each of the programs, they are\r\n   381\tdescribed here.  (In fact, every GNU program accepts (or should accept)\r\n   382\tthese options.)\r\n   383\t\r\n   384\t   Normally options and operands can appear in any order, and programs\r\n   385\tact as if all the options appear before any operands.  For example,\r\n   386\t‘sort -r passwd -t :’ acts like ‘sort -r -t : passwd’, since ‘:’ is an\r\n   387\toption-argument of ‘-t’.  However, if the ‘POSIXLY_CORRECT’ environment\r\n   388\tvariable is set, options must appear before operands, unless otherwise\r\n   389\tspecified for a particular command.\r\n   390\t\r\n   391\t   A few programs can usefully have trailing operands with leading ‘-’.\r\n   392\tWith such a program, options must precede operands even if\r\n   393\t‘POSIXLY_CORRECT’ is not set, and this fact is noted in the program\r\n   394\tdescription.  Fo"]
[15.411028, "o", "r example, the ‘env’ command’s options must appear\r\n   395\tbefore its operands, since in some cases the operands specify a command\r\n   396\tthat itself contains options.\r\n   397\t\r\n   398\t   Most programs that accept long options recognize unambiguous\r\n   399\tabbreviations of those options.  For example, ‘rmdir\r\n   400\t--ignore-fail-on-non-empty’ can be invoked as ‘rmdir --ignore-fail’ or\r\n   401\teven ‘rmdir --i’.  Ambiguous options, such as ‘ls --h’, are identified\r\n   402\tas such.\r\n   403\t\r\n   404\t   Some of these programs recognize the ‘--help’ and ‘--version’ options\r\n   405\tonly when one of them is the sole command line argument.  For these\r\n   406\tprograms, abbreviations of the long options are not always recognized.\r\n   407\t\r\n   408\t‘--help’\r\n   409\t     Print a usage message listing all available options, then exit\r\n   410\t     successfully.\r\n   411\t\r\n   412\t‘--version’\r\n   413\t     Print the version number, then exit successfully.\r\n   414\t\r\n   415\t‘--’\r\n   416\t"]
[15.411032, "o", "     Delimit the option list.  Later arguments, if any, are treated as\r\n   417\t     operands even if they begin with ‘-’.  For example, ‘sort -- -r’\r\n   418\t     reads from the file named ‘-r’.\r\n   419\t\r\n   420\t   A single ‘-’ operand is not really an option, though it looks like\r\n   421\tone.  It stands for a file operand, and some tools treat it as standard\r\n   422\tinput, or as standard output if that is clear from the context.  For\r\n   423\texample, ‘sort -’ reads from standard input, and is equivalent to plain\r\n   424\t‘sort’.  Unless otherwise specified, a ‘-’ can appear as any operand\r\n   425\tthat requires a file name.\r\n   426\t\r\n   427\t* Menu:\r\n   428\t\r\n   429\t* Exit status::                 Indicating program success or failure.\r\n   430\t* Backup options::              -b -S, in some programs.\r\n   431\t* Block size::                  BLOCK_SIZE and –block-size, in some programs.\r\n   432\t* Floating point::              Floating point number representation.\r\n   433\t* Signal speci"]
[15.411036, "o", "fications::       Specifying signals using the –signal option.\r\n   434\t* Disambiguating names and IDs:: chgrp, chown, chroot, id: user and group syntax\r\n   435\t* Random sources::              –random-source, in some programs.\r\n   436\t* Target directory::            Specifying a target directory, in some programs.\r\n   437\t* Trailing slashes::            –strip-trailing-slashes, in some programs.\r\n   438\t* Traversing symlinks::         -H, -L, or -P, in some programs.\r\n   439\t* Treating / specially::        –preserve-root and –no-preserve-root.\r\n   440\t* Special built-in utilities::  ‘break’, ‘:’, ...\r\n   441\t* Standards conformance::       Conformance to the POSIX standard.\r\n   442\t* Multi-call invocation::       Multi-call program invocation.\r\n   443\t\r\n   444\t2.1 Exit status\r\n   445\t===============\r\n   446\t\r\n   447\tNearly every command invocation yields an integral “exit status” that\r\n   448\tcan be used to change how other commands work.  For the vast majority of\r\n   449\tcommands, an exi"]
[15.411039, "o", "t status of zero indicates success.  Failure is\r\n   450\tindicated by a nonzero value—typically ‘1’, though it may differ on\r\n   451\tunusual platforms as POSIX requires only that it be nonzero.\r\n   452\t\r\n   453\t   However, some of the programs documented here do produce other exit\r\n   454\tstatus values and a few associate different meanings with the values ‘0’\r\n   455\tand ‘1’.  Here are some of the exceptions: ‘chroot’, ‘env’, ‘expr’,\r\n   456\t‘nice’, ‘nohup’, ‘numfmt’, ‘printenv’, ‘sort’, ‘stdbuf’, ‘test’,\r\n   457\t‘timeout’, ‘tty’.\r\n"]
